## Notes - mechanics workshop

### Players 
2, hobbyist/casual

### What we tested: 
Full play through

### Feedback
- It was fun
- We understood the concepts and mechanics relatively quickly
- Some wording or information was confusing (has been noted directly on the cards/boards)
- We quickly found exploits
- Events got easier to deal with - consider introducing stacking of events so the team has to deal with more and more every week?
- We didn't really use Standing
- As time went on we made the decision to ignore the skills and just focus on delivering features, partly because we didn't understand the point system and that you need to develop skills as well as deliver features

### Rule changes made as a result
Remember to explain the point system at the start of the game

Considered introducing event stacking, but does that make sense in a real life context? 
Isn't it a good thing that events get easier to deal with, because it shows the effect of the team improving skills? 
If we want to keep it to the same number of events per week then other things could be adjusted instead, e.g. how much work each feature requires

Evaluated which events should be included in week 1
Plan does nothing in week 1, but can we start work on a feature without a plan? A possible solution is that Feature A is always a scoping feature or similar (plan is wasted on Feature A anyway)
Decided on Work, Learn, Celebrate

Does it make sense to include Standing?
It could be used in balancing when it comes to final scoring

Activities Workshop and Align - what's the difference? Can they be made more distinct?
Align could be used to +1 a habit that's in the zone where there's a negative effect (and no +1 if it's higher up on the track)
That change will make things easier, so it needs to be balanced with other things as discussed with event stacking

Wording like "the first Work card" could be workshopped more, we'll keep an eye one it in subsequent play tests

We went through specific event cards but it's hard to judge whether they're overpowered without seeing them used in a play test
We'll watch and record how the event cards work in subsequent play tests 
We should avoid having events that are dependent on a specific activity card being played during the week, or make sure that a lot of copies of them are found in the event deck
